'' 'integer<N>' and 'uinteger<N>' did not parse at all -- 'expected an identifier' at
'' the '<' -- so numbers/integer_n.bas and fbc-int/array-size.bas, which are written
'' entirely in terms of them, failed at their first declaration. Measured with
'' '#print typeof': they are ALIASES, BYTE/SHORT/LONG/LONGINT and the unsigned row,
'' and not types of their own.
''
'' The VAL family has a WIDE half with its own five entry points, taking a bare
'' uint16* rather than a descriptor: fb_WstrVal, fb_WstrValInt, fb_WstrValUInt,
'' fb_WstrValLng and fb_WstrValULng. string/val.bas asks every one of the five of a
'' wstring beside its narrow twin, and all of them declined.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/sized-int-val.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_WstrValLng
'' GOLDEN: expected/sized-int-val.out
